From 78e4b51f4c001b25fa537821e626cbbb941fbd3f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 23:14:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Rotation matrices kept by the materials

The integrations by batches and the rotations of the values in the global
frame computed the rotation matrices of an orthotropic material on the host
at each call, and copied them to the device: one matrix per point for
material axes given point by point (12 million points per GPU process for a
polycrystal of 400 grains on 4 MI300A, as long as the integration itself),
and one small copy per material and per integration for a uniform rotation.

Material::getRotationMatrices keeps the matrices of computeRotationMatrices,
computed at the first call following setRotationMatrix; the kept array is
copied once to the device. Same values: identical outputs of the
polycrystal of mm-opera-hpc on the CPU.

Material axes given by partial quadrature functions are shared with the
material. If their values are modified in place afterwards,
Material::invalidateRotationMatrices must be called (setRotationMatrix
calls it): the matrices are computed again at their next use. Otherwise,
the integrations by batches go on with the matrices of the former axes,
whereas the integration point by point reads the axes at each point.

BatchIntegrationTest: material axes modified in place after a first
integration, in 3D (two directions given by functions) and in plane strain
(one direction). The integration by batches keeps the former matrices
until their invalidation, then gives the stresses of the integration point
by point; the kept matrices, transposed ones included, are the ones of the
current axes; the gradients in the global frame do not change. With the
behaviours of the generic and generic-parallel interfaces.
